# Owners are the team @graphty-org/code-owners, which does not exist yet, on purpose (owner's decision,
# 2026-10-25): until code-owner review is turned on -- the last step of the channels pipeline rollout --
# GitHub requests no review from it, so the owner gets no review-request email for every agent pull
# request. tools/check-owned-closure.mjs reads only the path patterns, which stay;
# tools/pr-integrity.mjs counts the owner's approval through its OWNER constant, not this file.
# Turning code-owner review on creates the team (with the owner in it) together with the ruleset change.
#
# What only the owner merges: the files that decide whether a pull request passes (a pull request runs its
# own copy of them, so a change here would judge itself), the screenshot gate, the release and the agents'
# own guards (design/ci/channels-pipeline.md, section 6.2). Everything else merges on green. It takes effect
# once the master ruleset requires a code owner's review; until then a pull request that changes an owned
# path auto-merges on green like any other (owner, 2026-10-25), except the few files that guard
# the owner, which tools/pr-open.sh holds (GUARD_PATHS).
#
# Owned closure: tools/check-owned-closure.mjs fails when an owned script or workflow reaches an
# unowned file through its imports or the paths it runs; such a file is added below, in its own
# pull request. Test files are deliberately not owned (the integrity check covers them), and so is
# tools/tool-tests.txt, the tool test list: the integrity check flags a line removed from it.

